<!-- Shared "How the score is computed" help button + modal — included from both
     economic.html.j2 and strength.html.j2 right after the page's legend
     paragraph, so the content lives in exactly one place (see also
     static/help-scoring.js for the shared open/close wiring). Reuses
     .modal/.modal-backdrop/.modal-content/.modal-close from static/style.css —
     same pattern as #econDetailModal (X, backdrop click, Escape all close it). -->
<p class="help-scoring-trigger" data-htr>
  <button type="button" id="helpScoringBtn" class="help-scoring-link" aria-haspopup="dialog">
    How the score is computed <span aria-hidden="true">?</span>
  </button>
</p>

<div id="helpScoringModal" class="modal" hidden>
  <div class="modal-backdrop"></div>
  <div class="modal-content help-scoring-content">
    <button class="modal-close" type="button" aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
    <h2>How the score is computed</h2>

    <p>Each indicator: the surprise (Actual &minus; Forecast) compared with its last 12 surprises.
    Far beyond its usual size &rarr; &plusmn;2, above average &rarr; &plusmn;1, normal &rarr; 0. Rows without a
    forecast, or too old, do not enter.</p>

    <p><b>Currency score.</b> Macro = the mean of the growth, inflation and labour category averages,
    each divided by that currency&rsquo;s usual spread; Carry = its policy rate (latest central-bank
    decision) minus the 8-currency mean; COT = speculative positioning. Score = 51% Macro + 34% Carry
    + 15% COT, each in its own &sigma; units (a missing block is left out, the others rescaled).</p>

    <p><b>Pairs.</b> (base &minus; quote) &times; 2.5; the US Dollar row uses the DXY positioning.
    <b>Indices</b>: 67% Macro + 33% Rates (the 3-month change of the home 2Y and US real 10Y
    yields; rising yields only erase a positive macro). <b>Metals</b>: 67% Rates (the 3-month change of the US 2Y
    yield) + 33% US Macro.</p>

    <p><b>Score and bias</b>: the raw score divided by that instrument&rsquo;s usual size (its RMS over
    3 years) gives z, shown as a Score from &minus;10 to +10 &mdash; 0&ndash;2 Neutral, 3&ndash;5 Bullish/Bearish,
    6&ndash;10 Very (about 55% of weeks are Neutral, 10% Very). <b>Strength</b> uses the macro part only.</p>

    <p>N = how many indicators actually entered. A small N = less confidence.</p>
  </div>
</div>
